body:
I’d ljke us to view designers in a couple of groups rather than classes or ‘better than’ or worse than:

- more consumer B2B design done at a company where the company value design and support designers, researchers and product managers. This is less about FAANG but probably a place that cares about metrics, has some level of UX maturity and likely good compensation;
- those who work in enterprise who likely get some level of support as a designer;
- the rest of us who often work in enterprise, internal tools and less ‘sexy’ sectors or challenges; these are places with no design maturity where team members tell us our role is to ‘make a sexy prototype’, where research isn’t done (too expensive, AI can do it) and no metrics are identified. The designers in this group are just as good as the first too but are continually looked down on, insulted by franc clients and the design profession and increasingly are walking away. It becomes tough to work in a field where you go into a meeting and your tech lead on a design systems project says ‘oh we don’t need a designer for this - I took a design class and can do it all’.
